  "portal_description": "Red Hat Subscription Asset Manager 1.3 is now available. This release adds\nseveral notable enhancements and fixes multiple bugs.\n\nRed Hat Subscription Asset Manager acts as an on-premise service to manage\nsubscription information and works as a proxy for software updates on\nclient machines.\n\nThis release includes these important new features:\n\n* Previously, subscriptions for physical systems were matched only for\nsocket counts. Now, subscriptions can also be matched based on cores or\nRAM.\n\n* Each product has an identifier with it that is used within the\nsubscription service to help track installed products. Subscription Asset\nManager now displays that product ID and product versions for a system on\na new \"Content\" tab for systems.\n\n* A new \"Refresh\" button allows any updated manifest to be downloaded from\nthe Portal automatically without ever having to log into the Customer\nPortal.\n\n* Systems can now be organized into system groups. System groups provide a\nconsistent way to organize systems within an organization and for features\nsuch as activation keys and access control. IMPORTANT: System groups\nreplace environments. Any existing environments in the organization will\nbe removed when SAM is upgraded.\n\n* The way that subscription pools are calculated has been updated to\nprovide more flexibility in subscription relationships to each other and to\nthe system to which they are attached. This is called \"instanced-based\nsubscriptions,\" and each unit which requires a subscription is an instance.\nA virtual system is an instance, whereas for physical system instances can\nbe a socket pair, cores, or RAM. For physical systems, subscriptions are\nconsumed from the pool in a set increment called the instance multiplier,\nwhich has a value of 2. The pool is dynamically calculated by taking the\nnumber of total subscriptions and multiplying it by the instance\nmultiplier, then applying it to systems. If there are 15 subscriptions\npurchased, there are 30 subscriptions in the pool (15 x 2). IMPORTANT: If\nany subscriptions will be purchased after October 1, 2013, those\nsubscriptions will use the new instance-based model. All SAM servers must\nbe upgraded to version 1.3 to use those new subscriptions. Also, it is\nstrongly recommended that the Red Hat Subscription Manager packages also be\nupdated on the client systems.\n\nSubscription Asset Manager 1.3 also includes two significant tech preview\nfeatures:\n\n* This version of SAM introduces new reporting packages which can return\ninformation on multiple organizations at once, rather than a single\norganization. Additionally, this enhanced reporting can be used to\nintegrate Satellite 5.6 and SAM 1.3 systems and to provide more\nfine-grained reports for Satellite systems.\n\n* Now, subordinate or chained distributors can be created underneath a\nmaster Subscription Asset Manager organization. This is essentially an\ninstance within an instance. The parent organization attaches\nsubscriptions to the child organization and creates a manifest on-premise\nrather than requiring all SAM servers to be registered with the Customer\nPortal.\n",
